      "A good design pays for itself before installation begins. It turns the wish for cameras and a fire system into exact numbers: how many detectors the codes demand for the area, which cameras cover the till and the perimeter without blind spots, where the routes run and precisely what goes into the specification. The client stops buying equipment with a just-in-case margin and paying for rework — the most expensive metres of cable are always the ones laid twice.",
      "Fire alarm is the field where a design is mandatory by law, not just common sense. Automatic fire alarm and evacuation systems are designed to Uzbekistan's national codes; without working documentation neither the expert review nor the fire inspectorate will accept the site. We calculate detector types and placement by room category, notification zones and start-up algorithms — and build in what inspectors look for first: release of escape routes and control of building services in a fire.",
      "In CCTV the design solves a different problem — picture quality rather than box-ticking. For every camera we calculate the view zone and pixel density per metre: one resolution spots a person in a yard, quite another reads a licence plate or a banknote at the till. Storage and network get their own maths: retention days turn into terabytes of disks, and camera counts into the PoE budget of switches.",
